; mdx runs the OCaml fences, except those marked skip: installation commands,
; which need the network, and the glue over rig, which needs a GPU. Shell
; examples (`munin`, `jq`, `tail`) are marked skip: they name run ids drawn
; per run and print what the reader's store holds. The guides read a crashed
; run, which a forked child stands for, and the machine's memory, which only
; Linux and macOS give together: Windows has no fork and names no boot, and
; munin reads neither a boot nor memory on other systems, such as FreeBSD.

(mdx
 (files *.md)
 (package munin)
 (enabled_if
  (or
   (= %{os} linux)
   (= %{os} macos)))
 (preludes prelude.ml)
 (libraries munin munin.sys unix))
